<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Listen! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's a School Day!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Listen!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Recognize basic familiar words supported with illustrations or visual aids. reading_2: Attempt to read basic and familiar words supported with pictures. phonemic_awareness: Identify sounds at the beginning of words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can recognize familiar words about classroom language supported with pictures (e.g., the word 'window' supported with a picture). Can attempt to read basic familiar words with picture support during chants, rhymes, or shared reading (e.g., 'cat' with a picture of a cat). Can identify the initial sound of a familiar word when heard, with the support of a picture (e.g., sound /b/ with the word 'book' supported with a picture of a book).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by playing a familiar "Good Morning Song" where students stand up, listen, and perform simple actions. The teacher then reviews classroom vocabulary from the previous lesson using large flashcards. For example, the teacher holds up a picture of a 'book' and says, "Good morning, everyone! What is this? It is a book!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to recognize a word. The teacher holds up a flashcard with the picture of a 'book' and the word 'book' written clearly below it. The teacher points to the picture and says, "This is a **book**." Then, the teacher points to the word 'book' and says, "This word says **book**." The teacher repeats for 'window' and 'door', emphasizing the initial sounds /b/, /l/, /t/ by slightly exaggerating the articulation: "/b/ook", "/l/isten", "/t/ouch". The teacher then asks students to mimic the actions and sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a chair) "Is this a book?" (Expected answer: No.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a door) "What is this?" (Expected answer: Door.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's a School Day!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> "Our Classroom Words" Big Book or large Picture-Word Chart. This material consists of large, clear pictures of classroom objects (chair, desk, table, window, door, book, teacher, student) with their corresponding words printed below them in large, easy-to-read font. The content presents simple sentences like "It is a door. Please open the door." and "It is a book. Let's listen." Genre: Simple Descriptive/Instructional. Register: Informal, child-friendly. Format: Big book/Picture chart.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Recognize basic familiar words supported with illustrations or visual aids. Attempt to read basic and familiar words supported with pictures. Identify sounds at the beginning of words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a picture of a 'window' in the Big Book and asks, "What is it?" Students respond. Then the teacher points to the word 'window' and asks, "Can you find the word 'window'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says, "Touch the **desk**!" Students point to the picture of the desk and the word 'desk' on the chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads sentences from the Big Book, e.g., "It is a book." and students point to the 'book' picture and word.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice recognizing and attempting to read target vocabulary words (chair, desk, table, book, window, door, teacher, student, listen, stand up, touch, open) with picture support. Grammar focus: Imperatives ('Open the book', 'Stand up'), Present simple ('It is a book'). Pronunciation focus: Initial sounds /b/ (book), /l/ (listen), /t/ (touch).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Read and Match:</b> Students receive individual worksheets with pictures of classroom items on one side and word cards (e.g., 'book', 'door', 'chair', 'window') on the other. The teacher calls out a word, "Find the word 'book'!" Students cut out the word 'book' and glue it next to the corresponding picture. The teacher circulates, providing support and checking accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Picture-Word Chant:</b> The teacher leads a simple chant using picture-word flashcards. For example: "I see a **book**! /b/, /b/, /b/ book!" (Teacher shows the 'book' flashcard, students repeat the word and sound while pointing to the word). "I see a **window**! /w/, /w/, /w/ window!" (Repeat for other target words like 'listen' /l/, 'touch' /t/, 'teacher', 'student'). This reinforces initial sounds and word recognition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair "Find the Word" Game:</b> Students work in pairs with a set of small picture-word cards (e.g., 'chair', 'desk', 'door'). One student hides the word part of a card and shows only the picture, asking, "What is it?" The other student attempts to read the word from the picture-card or finds the matching word from a separate pile of word-only cards. E.g., Student A: (shows picture of a chair) "What is it?" Student B: "It is a **chair**." The teacher monitors, providing polite prompts like "Please, listen carefully" and "Please, try to read."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's a School Day!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Classroom Reading Tour" – a guided reading activity. The teacher displays large labels (e.g., 'door', 'window', 'desk', 'chair', 'book') on actual classroom objects or on a large classroom poster. Students, in small groups or as a whole class, will go on a "reading tour" around the classroom or point to the poster, identifying and attempting to read the labels.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says, "Let's read our classroom words! Please, stand up and walk with me." (Pragmatic: Following instructions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to the 'door' label on the actual door and asks, "What is this?" Students collectively attempt to read the word 'door'. The teacher confirms and might say, "It is a **door**. Please, **open** the **door**." (Imperative, vocabulary, pronunciation /d/oor).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The activity continues with other classroom items: 'window', 'desk', 'chair', 'book'. For 'book', the teacher might say, "Look, it is a **book**. Let's **listen** to the sound /b/ in **book**."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns pointing and reading the words aloud, supported by the teacher and peers. The teacher encourages polite requests such as "Please, read the word."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[8-10 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist. The teacher observes individual students during the "Our Classroom Reading Tour" and a final word recognition game, noting their ability to recognize and attempt to read target words and identify initial sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Show Me the Word!" Game. The teacher holds up large picture flashcards for 'book', 'window', 'door', 'chair', 'desk'. For each picture, the teacher asks, "What is it?" and then holds up three word cards (e.g., 'book', 'cat', 'pen'). Students must point to the correct word card that matches the picture and attempt to say the word. The teacher also specifically asks, "What sound does 'book' start with?" for /b/, and "What sound does 'listen' start with?" for /l/ using the respective flashcards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes familiar words (e.g., 'book', 'window', 'door') supported with pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to read basic and familiar words with picture support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies the initial sound of a familiar word when heard (e.g., /b/ for 'book', /l/ for 'listen', /t/ for 'touch').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to simple WH-questions like "What is it?".</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show "thumbs up" if they can find the word 'door' on a flashcard when asked, and "thumbs down" if they need more help. The teacher can also ask, "Can you find the word 'book'?" and students point to it.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[7-8 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new classroom word did you read today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite picture-word to read?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What activities worked best to help students recognize familiar words with picture support?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully attempt to read the basic words? Were the initial sound activities effective?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolding might be needed for students who struggled with word recognition or initial sounds? How can I ensure all students are ready to *speak* about these words in the next lesson?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading familiar classroom words. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students actively speak and interact using these recognized words, asking and answering simple questions about classroom objects.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>